metrics-server scrapes the kubelet, so `kubectl top` and HorizontalPodAutoscaler
work. It talks to the kubelet over TLS with a certificate the kubelet signed for
itself, which no public CA vouches for, so --kubelet-insecure-tls is set. Every
local-cluster distribution does the same thing for the same reason.

  kubectl top nodes
  kubectl top pods -A

It needs about fifteen seconds after enabling before it has anything to report.

One more thing on ferry. `kubectl top` reads metrics.k8s.io, which is an
aggregated API: the API server does not answer it, metrics-server does, and the
API server has to reach that pod to ask. The API server is a macOS process and
is not on the pod network, so it cannot -- the request times out and kubectl
says "Metrics API not available".

Giving it a route means binding ClusterIPs on the Mac, which needs root:

  FERRY_HOST_CLUSTER_IPS=1 ferry up

Everything below metrics-server works without that: the kubelet serves real
container CPU and memory, which you can see directly with

  kubectl get --raw /api/v1/nodes/<node>/proxy/metrics/resource
